=== B1 probe: three cloud-density arms, same noise field ===

--- Part 1: lower edge in noise units, across coverage and rain ---
  coverage   rain |      visible    occlusion   reflection |      occ - vis     refl - vis
         0    0.0 |       0.4100       0.4100       0.3700 |       0.000000      -0.040000
         0    0.5 |       0.2700       0.2700       0.2300 |       0.000000      -0.040000
         0    1.0 |       0.1300       0.1300       0.0900 |       0.000000      -0.040000
        25    0.0 |       0.3100       0.3100       0.2700 |       0.000000      -0.040000
        25    0.5 |       0.1700       0.1700       0.1300 |       0.000000      -0.040000
        25    1.0 |       0.0300       0.0300      -0.0100 |       0.000000      -0.040000
        50    0.0 |       0.2100       0.2100       0.1700 |       0.000000      -0.040000
        50    0.5 |       0.0700       0.0700       0.0300 |       0.000000      -0.040000
        50    1.0 |      -0.0700      -0.0700      -0.1100 |      -0.000000      -0.040000
        75    0.0 |       0.1100       0.1100       0.0700 |       0.000000      -0.040000
        75    0.5 |      -0.0300      -0.0300      -0.0700 |      -0.000000      -0.040000
        75    1.0 |      -0.1700      -0.1700      -0.2100 |      -0.000000      -0.040000
       100    0.0 |       0.0100       0.0100      -0.0300 |       0.000000      -0.040000
       100    0.5 |      -0.1300      -0.1300      -0.1700 |      -0.000000      -0.040000
       100    1.0 |      -0.2700      -0.2700      -0.3100 |       0.000000      -0.040000

  => occlusion - visible is 0.000000 across the whole grid: the two arms sit on the
     SAME lower edge. The 0.04 in the ticket is the gap between the 0.25 base of
     the visible test and the 0.21 base of the occlusion test, which is exactly
     cancelled by the -0.04 that coverageBias already carries. Both numbers are
     real; they are the same number.

--- Part 2: which arm the 0.04 really separates ---
  visible start    = 0.2100
  occlusion start  = 0.2100
  reflection start = 0.1700
  => the reflection board is the arm that is 0.04 low, and that is the A6 finding
     (overworld.glsl:64-66 vs clouds_planar.glsl:225), not an occlusion mismatch.

--- Part 3: edge width, measured on the quantity each arm actually shapes ---
  visible cloud  : a hard `continue` on the march sample; the rendered edge width
                   is the march step planeDistanceDif/sampleCount (6..64 samples
                   across the 18-block sheet), i.e. roughly 0.3..3 blocks of world
                   space - NOT a 0.22-wide smoothstep.
  occlusion arm  : smoothstep over +/-0.025 = 0.05 noise units.
  reflection     : smoothstep over 0.22 noise units  <- this is where the 0.22 in
                   the ticket comes from.
  => the '4.4x harder' comparison took the reflection board's 0.22 as if it were the
     visible cloud's edge. Against the visible cloud's actual hard cut, the occlusion
     window is a softening, not a hardening.

Conclusion: 11.E-9's two premises do not hold against the current source, so no
change is made. The ticket's own acceptance line ('compare the shadow edge with the
cloud it comes from') stays valid for the CLOUD_SHADOWS option, which is off in all
three tiers; the reflection-board offset it does describe belongs to A6.
